# Lifecycle and History

Each resource manages one API identity. Parent schedule/calendar resources
manage settings independently of child objects. Entries and overrides created
outside Terraform remain untouched by parent settings updates.

## Destroy

| Resource family | Destroy behavior |
| --- | --- |
| Teams and services | Disable; retain UUIDs, links and reserved names |
| Memberships | Revoke the binding and retain history |
| Escalation chains and work calendars | Archive, retaining history; current dependencies can block archival |
| Escalation rules and alert filters | Disable the retained current definition |
| Schedule settings | Disable and retain history |
| Primary rotations and overrides | Retire the addressed plan with `mode=all`; retain immutable versions and historical references |
| Channel catalog bindings and calendar entries | Delete only the managed binding/entry |
| Integrations | Hide and revoke credentials, retaining alerts and historical links |
| Service accounts | Deactivate and revoke all keys; retain identity and role assignments |
| Role assignments | Revoke only the explicit binding; retain audit history |
| Team role defaults | Clear both tenant-wide member/lead role sets with the saved policy revision |
| Schedule handover | Save disabled settings with an empty channel set, including under inactive owners |
| Outgoing webhooks | Disable the definition and retain historical references |
| Bots and voice providers | Retire the connection and erase its credentials; retained channel/contact dependencies block retirement |
| Custom RBAC roles and groups | Delete only when unused; dependencies cause a conflict |
| Manual group memberships and OIDC mappings | Remove only the explicit managed source or mapping |
| Notification-template overrides | Delete the override and restore current inheritance |
| Silences | Delete the whole UUID/team set; retain the anonymous historical suppression fact |
| Inhibition rules | Delete live configuration; preserve captured holds and referenced versions |
| Enrichment datasets | Delete live configuration with body expected_version; current-rule dependencies can conflict |
| Enrichment rules | Publish an empty whole-tenant snapshot with saved expected_revision; retain history |

The provider reports failed mutations and retains state. It does not silently
forget active configuration, unlink dependencies or retry conflicting writes.
Remove current references explicitly when the API blocks retirement.

## Import and inactive state

Resource pages specify exact import IDs. Some use a UUID; scoped resources use
compound IDs such as `team_uuid/chain_uuid`. Names are not import identities.
Import reads only. A later apply reconciles the configuration, including default
`active = true` where supported. Use false explicitly to retain inactive state.

Team reactivation does not reactivate disabled child services/accounts/filters
or restore revoked credentials. An archived chain/calendar cannot be restored;
refresh treats it as absence for the managed active-definition resource.

Service accounts do not receive roles or tokens implicitly. Reactivation never
restores revoked keys. Manual group membership and OIDC-derived membership are
independent sources; deleting the manual source does not remove derived access.

Role assignment UUIDs stay fixed when their role, recipient or target changes.
Refresh accepts valid tuple changes made outside Terraform; apply reconciles the
configured values with an atomic expected-tuple comparison. Group-derived grants
remain group assignments, rather than separate per-user objects.

Team role defaults own the complete member and additional lead role sets across
the tenant. Import the literal ID `tenant` to adopt an existing nonempty policy.
Destroy clears both sets and removes those dynamic grants across all teams;
explicit assignments remain independent. No saved UI snapshot or guessed builtin
configuration is restored. Configure only one owner of this policy per tenant.

Notification-template resources own explicit overrides, not inherited effective
content. Each identity includes its explicit language (`ru` or `en`); inheritance
and override ETags are independent between languages. A successful read with no
stored override is absence. Permission or
transport failures retain state. Global template changes can affect all teams
that inherit them. Saving templates performs no test delivery or resend.

One-time integration tokens and stored polling secrets cannot be recovered by
import. Refer to the integration page before adopting an existing object.

Bot and voice imports recover public settings and secret key names. They cannot
recover saved credentials. Omitted secret actions retain stored values; explicit
replacement rotates a credential. Dependency conflicts retain the connection in
state. Set `enabled = false` explicitly when a referenced connection must remain
configured but inactive. The provider never removes contacts or catalog bindings
implicitly to permit retirement.

## Conflicts and time

Versioned resources use their own observed revision/ETag. A conflict requires a
fresh plan; the provider does not replace the expected revision automatically.
Custom roles use their numeric revision, OIDC mappings their own revision and
notification templates their strong SHA-256 slot ETag. Group metadata PATCH is
unversioned; its observed metadata revision is used only for deletion.
Team role defaults use their own numeric policy revision. Assignment updates
compare the saved complete tuple through body `expected`, with explicit nullable
target team values; they have no revision or If-Match. Stale assignments return
412, duplicate destinations409. The successful update receipt supplies state
directly, including after a self-demotion that removes future read authority.
Silence/inhibition use their own numeric If-Match. Enrichment uses body
expected_version/expected_revision and reports 409 conflicts. Dataset metadata
and rows share configuration_version; immutable row revision is not the write
version. Whole-tenant enrichment rules have one explicit owner.
Teams/services use unversioned field updates. Rule/filter publication appends
revisions without an If-Match precondition.
Bot and voice updates also have no public revision or ETag: full settings replace
the current configuration and can overwrite a concurrent edit. A successful
update is saved before a follow-up read; a failed read retains the committed
desired values for recovery.

Calendar weekly settings and recurring exception clocks use native dated views:
explicit fixed source references and dated interval sets. Source weekdays remain
UTC. Local datetimes use the provider request timezone; explicit RFC3339 offsets
remain absolute. Date-only fields retain their domain meaning.
Primary rotations require an explicit `effective_from` boundary, separate from
the recurrence anchor. Updates preserve earlier planned coverage; override
updates replace their entire one-off plan. Both use stable declaration reads
independent of request time and require whole-second local or explicit-offset
timestamps. The backend handles timezone interpretation. Local input preserves
server-confirmed instants across refresh; changing the explicit provider timezone
plans an update. Post-write reads cannot bind another actor's newer revision.
Use each resource's documented null/empty behavior when clearing settings.

## Current limitations

- Personal contact enrollment, verification and test delivery remain separate
  user actions. Shared bot, voice and outgoing-webhook resources configure
  connections; they do not register user contacts or discover destinations.
- Token rotation, target discovery and immediate polling are not implicit
  resource operations. Six data sources support explicit identity lookup; the
  permissions data source reads the native metadata catalog.
